Type Ctrl+] to exit the console.

Subtask 3.2: Performance Comparison Testing
Let's create a simple performance test to compare pre and post-migration performance.

Create a performance test script:
cat << 'EOF' > vm-performance-test.sh
#!/bin/bash

VM_NAME=${1:-migration-test-vm}
NAMESPACE=${2:-default}

echo "Performance Test for VM: $VM_NAME"
echo "=================================="

echo "Testing VM responsiveness..."
start_time=$(date +%s%N)
virtctl ssh fedora@$VM_NAME -n $NAMESPACE --command="echo 'VM Response Test'" 2>/dev/null
end_time=$(date +%s%N)
response_time=$(( (end_time - start_time) / 1000000 ))
echo "VM response time: ${response_time}ms"

echo ""
echo "Checking VM resource usage..."
oc top pod -l kubevirt.io/vm=$VM_NAME -n $NAMESPACE

echo ""
echo "Verifying VM network performance..."
virtctl ssh fedora@$VM_NAME -n $NAMESPACE --command="curl -s -w 'Total time: %{time_total}s\n' -o /dev/null http://httpbin.org/get" 2>/dev/null || echo "Network test skipped (VM may not have curl installed)"
EOF

chmod +x vm-performance-test.sh
Run performance test:
./vm-performance-test.sh migration-test-vm default
Subtask 3.3: Verify Storage and Data Integrity
Ensure that VM storage and data remain intact after migration.

Check PVC status:
oc get pvc -l kubevirt.io/vm=migration-test-vm
Verify VM disk usage:
virtctl ssh fedora@migration-test-vm --command="df -h" 2>/dev/null || echo "SSH not available, checking from host"
Test file system integrity (if SSH is available):
